            <common:generalComment xml:lang="en">Based on a solvent-based DAC facility with annual capture capacity of 1 Mt CO2 and lifetime of 20 years. Potassium hydroxide and sodium hydroxide are used to capture CO2. Solvent-based DACCS requires dedicated high-temperature (900 Â°C) heat for CO2 regeneration. Thus, from a thermodynamic perspective, heat supply options are largely limited to combusting energy dense fuels such as (renewable) natural gas or (renewable) hydrogen, while electric resistance heating and electrochemical regeneration approaches are in development. Includes compression, transport and underground storage of CO2. Source: Qiu, Y., Lamers, P., Daioglou, V. et al. Environmental trade-offs of direct air capture technologies in climate change mitigation toward 2100. Nat Commun 13, 3635 (2022). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-31146-1
UUID: 01db200b-d3a9-4299-849c-b2c9bafa4916</common:generalComment>
